.TH WIILAND-CONFIG 1 "September 2026" "wiiland" "WiiLand Manual"
.SH NAME
wiiland-config \- graphical WiiLand configuration and diagnostics
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wiiland-config
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wiiland-config
opens the WiiLand Control Center, a native eframe/egui application.  It edits
Wii Remote profiles and device rules, invokes
.BR wiilandd (1)
for offline configuration and diagnostics, captures live traces and motion
calibration through daemon IPC, and controls the per-user systemd service.
.PP
The initial window is 1180 by 780 pixels and cannot be resized below 760 by
600.  The application id is
.BR io.github.philosophimoonbeam.wiiland-config .
The native window backend is selected by eframe for the current session.
Overview's configuration details report Wayland when WAYLAND_DISPLAY is set,
X11 when DISPLAY is set otherwise, and unknown when neither is set.
.PP
The frontend does not create input devices or run a private daemon.  All
commands run asynchronously, with their standard output and standard error
shown in the expandable Activity log.
.SH CONFIGURATION FILES
On startup the frontend selects the user path
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
when XDG_CONFIG_HOME is absolute.  Otherwise, when HOME is absolute, it uses
.B $HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf .
If neither environment variable yields an absolute path, the daemon system
path
.B /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
is used as the editable target.
.PP
The initial load runs
.B wiilandd --dump-config
without
.B --config
for the default user target, so the daemon's optional system file is layered
before its optional user file.  If a different path is entered, config-
sensitive commands receive
.B --config
with that path and use that file alone.  The entered path is also the save
target.  Device listing and axis-map commands do not receive a config path.
.PP
Configuration values use the daemon's canonical key=value tokens.  See
.BR wiilandd (1)
for all accepted keys, ranges, defaults, and layering rules.
.SH APPEARANCE AND NAVIGATION
Pearl uses a warm neutral canvas, opaque light cards, evergreen text, and teal
actions.  Dusk uses layered graphite surfaces and sea-glass accents.
Appearance selects Follow system, Pearl, or Dusk for this session.
The numbered sidebar becomes horizontal navigation below 1000 pixels wide.
Page content scrolls independently from the persistent save controls and
Activity log.  Selected, hovered, focused, and disabled controls have distinct
visual treatments; neutral status labels do not imply success.
.SH OVERVIEW
Overview puts service status and device discovery first, with a short
Connect / Configure / Test sequence.  Configuration file & advanced settings
contains the editable configuration path, daemon executable, and detected
window system.  Custom files are identified explicitly because the background
service does not load them.
Controls are:
.TP
.B Refresh
Run
.BR "systemctl --user is-active wiilandd.service" .
.TP
.B Refresh daemon status
Query the daemon over IPC and show its running configuration and reactor
timing/loss counters in the Activity log.
.TP
.B Start , Stop , Restart
Run the corresponding
.BR "systemctl --user"
operation for
.BR wiilandd.service .
The primary service action is Start or Restart according to the queried state;
the Service actions menu contains all three operations.
.TP
.B Check readiness
Run
.B "wiilandd --doctor"
\&.
.TP
.B Find devices
Run
.BR "wiilandd --list --verbose" .
.PP
A service status query distinguishes Running, Stopped, Failed, and
Unavailable.  Successful service changes trigger a fresh status query.
.SH CONFIGURE
Configure separates settings into four focused pages:
.TP
.B Profile & pointer
Choose Gamepad, Desktop, or Both, then tune Pointer response: D-pad pointer
speed, IR gain, IR jitter deadzone, and IR smoothing.  Advanced IR mapping &
screen calibration expands to show IR tracking (dual, centroid, or first),
IR aim mapping (relative or absolute), and optional screen calibration bounds.
.TP
.B Motion aiming
Choose Off, Right stick, or Mouse pointer.  Response groups sensitivity,
deadzone, smoothing, and X/Y inversion.  Sensor & activation groups the sensor
(automatic, IR, MotionPlus, or accelerometer) and activation (always, B,
Nunchuk Z, or Nunchuk C).  Saved sensor calibration expands to show independent
accelerometer and MotionPlus calibration toggles and X/Y/Z fields, with
calibration duration.
.TP
.B Button bindings
Desktop buttons assigns actions to Wii Remote A, B, +, -, Home, 1, and 2.
Each action is one of disabled, left-click, right-click, enter, escape, overview,
page-up, or page-down.  These mappings apply to Desktop and Both; Gamepad
retains its standard button mapping.
.TP
.B Device rules
Controller rules supports up to 32 ordered rules.  Each rule matches a
substring of either the device path or device type and selects Gamepad,
Desktop, or Both.  Rules are evaluated from top to bottom; the last matching
rule wins.  Move earlier lowers a rule's priority and Move later raises it;
movement is disabled at the first and last boundaries respectively.
Add rule appends a rule, and Remove deletes the selected rule.
.PP
Numeric widgets constrain values to the daemon's accepted ranges.  Changes are
marked unsaved.  Validation errors appear above the save controls.  The save
bar remains visible while scrolling and on every page, with or without edits.
Its controls are:
.TP
.B Reload
Reload effective daemon configuration.  Unsaved edits require confirmation
before they are discarded.
.TP
.B Validate and save
Validate the form with wiilandd --check-config against a same-directory
temporary file.  On success, atomically replace the selected target; on
failure, preserve the existing file.
.TP
.B Save and restart
Available only for the default user target.  Save as above and then run
.B "systemctl --user restart wiilandd.service"
\&.  It is disabled for an
explicit target because the installed service does not load that target.
.PP
Ctrl+S validates and saves pending edits without restarting the service.
.SH TEST AND CALIBRATE
Test & calibrate accepts an optional device selector (a positive ordinal or
sysfs path) and event filter (all, keys, axes, ir, or motion-plus).
By default it captures through the running daemon using IPC 1.1, while normal
virtual input remains active. Refresh daemon status shows the running
configuration and reactor timing/loss counters in the activity log.
.TP
.B Start trace
Lease readable sensors for the selected daemon device, or all devices present
at capture startup when no selector is given. Start a new session for newly
connected controllers.
.TP
.B Stop capture
Cancel capture and close its connection, releasing its sensor leases.
.TP
.B Capture calibration
Collect samples for the configured duration. An empty selector chooses the
first daemon device. Stable accelerometer and MotionPlus values are computed
independently, applied to the form, and marked unsaved. Device loss, interface
changes, and queue overflow reject the capture.
.TP
.B Direct hardware diagnostics
Expand Advanced capture options to enable this explicit mode with the daemon
stopped for standalone subprocess capture.  Traces run with --dry-run and
--no-ipc; temporary profiles apply only in this mode.
Calibration uses --calibrate-aim.
.PP
Trace and calibration are mutually exclusive.  Capture selectors are locked
while a capture is running.  Stop capture remains available in the bottom
status strip when a capture is active, including on other pages.
.PP
The collapsed Saved-file diagnostics & reference section provides Check readiness
(--doctor), Validate saved file (--check-config), Effective settings
(--dump-config), Input map (--axis-map), and Test checklist
(--validation-checklist).  These commands read saved configuration, not
pending edits.  The daemon's test checklist covers controller families and
desktop/game compatibility checks.
.SH ACTIVITY LOG
The resizable Activity log displays commands, stdout, stderr, and process
errors, with wrapped text and vertical scrolling.  It opens for explicit
diagnostic commands, captures, and configuration failures.  The bottom status
strip's Activity log toggle includes the retained block count.  Copy all copies
the complete retained text; Clear removes it; Hide log collapses the drawer.
The buffer retains at most 10,000 text blocks.  Completion results that
belong to an old form revision or target are discarded, and a failed save
preserves existing data.
.PP
Closing or reloading with unsaved changes opens a modal confirmation.
Discard changes continues the requested action; Keep editing or Escape
returns to the form without discarding edits.
.SH PERMISSIONS
The frontend does not elevate privileges.  The selected configuration target
and its parent must be writable to save.  Service controls require a working
systemd user manager.  The daemon itself requires access to hid-wiimote input
nodes and /dev/uinput; permission errors are reported in the Activity log.
.SH EXIT STATUS
The application exits successfully when its eframe window closes normally.  A
nonzero status indicates that native window initialization or another eframe
runtime operation failed.  A failed daemon command, validation, trace, or
service operation is reported in the window and does not by itself terminate
the frontend.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
Canonical installed system configuration path.
.TP
.B $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
Default per-user configuration and save target when absolute.
.TP
.B $HOME/.config/wiiland/wiilandd.conf
Fallback per-user configuration and save target.
.TP
.B wiilandd.service
Per-user systemd unit controlled by the Overview tab.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wiilandd (1),
.BR wiiland-show (1),
.BR wiiland (7),
.BR systemctl (1)
